  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">424.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;VLM_LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">25.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-724331b1e2f5105ca6162abeddd355d2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0ff5c99c56fa54fe27e56cb06863931&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;After starting the lightrag-server, you can add an Ollama-type connection in the Open WebUI admin panel. And then a model named `lightrag:latest` will appear in Open WebUI's model management interface. Users can then send queries to LightRAG through the chat interface. You should install LightRAG as a service for this use case.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;VLM_MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=5242880&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">10.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The API key is passed using the request header `X-API-Key`. Below is an example of accessing the LightRAG Server via API:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;SURROUNDING_LEADING_MAX_TOKENS=2000&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">10.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To enable secure access control, the following environment variables are required:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;SURROUNDING_TRAILING_MAX_TOKENS=2000&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">10.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The command prompts for the password and prints an `admin:{bcrypt}...` entry ready to paste into `.env'.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;# Optional reranker configuration&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">10.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Currently, only the configuration of an administrator account and password is supported.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;# VLM configuration for documents using i/t/e process options&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If Account credentials are not configured, the Web UI will access the system as a Guest.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Different instances access the same database instance, you need to use workspaces to achieve data isolation; otherwise, the data of different instances will conflict and be destroyed.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Even if only an API Key is configured, all APIs can still be accessed through the Guest account, which remains insecure.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The command-line `workspace` argument and the `WORKSPACE` environment variable in the `.env` file can both be used to specify the workspace name for the current instance, with the command-line argument having higher priority.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Hence, to safeguard the API, it is necessary to configure both authentication methods simultaneously.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Here is how workspaces are implemented for different types of storage: for local file-based databases, data isolation is achieved through workspace subdirectories; for databases that store data in collections, it's done by adding a workspace prefix to the collection name; for Qdrant vector database, data isolation is achieved through payload-based partitioning (Qdrant's recommended multitenancy approach); for relational databases, data isolation is achieved by adding a `workspace` field to the tables for logical data separation; for graph databases, logical data isolation is achieved through labels; for OpenSearch, data isolation is achieved through index name prefixes.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;ERANK_BINDING=aliyun&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For relational databases, data isolation is achieved by adding a `workspace` field to the tables for logical data separation.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;RERANK_MODEL=gte-rerank-v2&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For graph databases, logical data isolation is achieved through labels.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">18.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;RERANK_BINDING_HOST=https://dashscope.aliyuncs.com/api/v1/services/rerank/text-rerank/text-rerank&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For OpenSearch, data isolation is achieved through index name prefixes.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">18.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A local reranker that exposes a Cohere-compatible API can be used by setting the appropriate environment variables. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is important to remember that the LightRAG Server will load the environment variables from `.env` into the system environment variables at each startup, affecting how the server operates.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG now supports configurable index types for Milvus vector storage (AUTOINDEX, HNSW, HNSW_SQ, IVF_FLAT, etc.) through environment variables. HNSW_SQ requires Milvus 2.6.8+ and provides significant memory savings. See the &quot;Using Milvus for Vector Storage&quot; section in the main README.md for complete configuration options.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Setting appropriate limits at both layers ensures better error messages and security.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For the MinerU official API, the MINERU_API_MODE must be set to 'official' and the MINERU_API_TOKEN must be provided. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;VS Code users with Python extensions should be aware that the integrated terminal may automatically load the `.env` file, requiring a new terminal session after any changes.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;You can select the storage implementation by configuring environment variables. For instance, prior to the initial launch of the API server, you can set the following environment variable to specify your desired storage implementation:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If you want to build up your own offline environment, please refer to [Offline Deployment Guide](./OfflineDeployment.md).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The VLM_LLM_MODEL parameter specifies the Language Model to be used for Vision and Language Model processing. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To configure different LLMs/VLMs for various tasks like entity extraction, keyword extraction, or multimodal analysis, refer to the Role-Specific LLM/VLM Configuration Guide.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Data migration from one storage implementation to another is not supported yet. For further information, please read the sample `.env.example` file.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The first way is to configure a completely independent working environment for each instance.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The LightRAG Server is designed to provide a Web UI and API support. The Web UI facilitates document indexing, knowledge graph exploration, and a simple RAG query interface. LightRAG Server also provides an Ollama-compatible interface, aiming to emulate LightRAG as an Ollama chat model. This allows AI chat bots, such as Open WebUI, to access LightRAG easily.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Examples of common settings for LLM and Embedding models are provided to illustrate how to configure these parameters.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When switching the storage implementation in LightRAG, the LLM cache can be migrated from the existing storage to the new one. Subsequently, when re-uploading files to the new storage, the pre-existing LLM cache will significantly accelerate file processing. For detailed instructions on using the LLM cache migration tool, please refer to [README_MIGRATE_LLM_CACHE.md](../lightrag/tools/README_MIGRATE_LLM_CACHE.md)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;This requires creating a separate working directory for each instance and placing a dedicated `.env` configuration file in that directory.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The Web UI facilitates document indexing, knowledge graph exploration, and a simple RAG query interface.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When targeting Google Gemini, the `LLM_BINDING` and `LLM_MODEL` should be set accordingly, and your Gemini key must be provided.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG API Server Command Line Options&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">18.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The server listening ports in the configuration files of different instances cannot be the same.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG Server also provides an Ollama-compatible interface, aiming to emulate LightRAG as an Ollama chat model. This allows AI chat bots, such as Open WebUI, to access LightRAG easily.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The example for Ollama LLM + Ollama Embedding demonstrates how to configure these models.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Reranking Configuration&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Then, you can start the service by running `lightrag-server` in the working directory.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The embedding model and asymmetric embedding configuration must be decided before document indexing and must remain consistent during the query phase.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;This allows AI chat bots, such as Open WebUI, to access LightRAG easily.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Reranking query-recalled chunks can significantly enhance retrieval quality by re-ordering documents based on an optimized relevance scoring model. LightRAG currently supports the following rerank providers:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The second way is for all instances to share the same set of `.env` configuration files.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The v1.5.0rc2 release adds the new file-processing pipeline, parser routing, multimodal analysis, role-specific LLM/VLM configuration, JSON entity extraction, and several provider/storage changes. Review the [v1.5.0rc2 release notes](https://github.com/HKUDS/LightRAG/releases/tag/v1.5.0rc2) before upgrading a production instance.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The vector dimension must be defined upon initial table creation for certain storage solutions like PostgreSQL.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Cohere / vLLM**: Offers full API integration with Cohere AI's `v2/rerank` endpoint. As vLLM provides a Cohere-compatible reranker API, all reranker models deployed via vLLM are also supported.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;and then use command-line arguments to specify different server listening ports and workspaces for each instance.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To keep the old file-processing behavior while upgrading the server, set: `&lt;code&gt;LIGHTRAG_PARSER=*:legacy-F&lt;/code&gt;`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When changing configurations, it's necessary to clear the existing LightRAG workspace and re-index the source files.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Jina AI**: Provides complete implementation compatibility with all Jina rerank models.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;You can start multiple LightRAG instances in the same working directory with different command-line arguments.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`ENTITY_TYPES` is no longer supported. Use `ENTITY_TYPE_PROMPT_FILE` instead, with a YAML profile stored under `PROMPT_DIR/entity_type` (`PROMPT_DIR` defaults to `./prompts`). A sample template is available at `prompts/samples/entity_type_prompt.sample.yml`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The Asymmetric Embedding Configuration section details how to enable and configure asymmetric embeddings.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Aliyun**: Features a custom implementation designed to support Aliyun's rerank API format.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-44dde1b8fa9d74e9edbda4a6a1d22019</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For example:">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If you use OpenSearch storage and the cluster is older than OpenSearch 3.3.0, upgrade OpenSearch before enabling the v1.5 storage path and validate existing indices. For new deployments, use OpenSearch 3.3.0 or later.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The full validation rules and examples for asymmetric embedding can be found in the Asymmetric Embedding Configuration document.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The rerank provider is configured via the `.env` file. Below is an example configuration for a rerank model deployed locally using vLLM:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
