<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Boundary ambiguity: a body line that genuinely begins with `#␠` would be misjudged as a heading line — this is the same heuristic ambiguity already documented and accepted in `lightrag/parser/_markdown.py`, with very low probability in real corpora.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The overlap behaviours scattered across stages are gathered here:">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;↓  HeadingGlue: glue body-less heading blocks forward into their strictly-deeper child             → §3.5 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE` | Unset (inherits `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE`) &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;1. Encode the bridge text into tokens.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`mineru`: detects headings by an item's `text_level &gt; 0` or `label` being `title` / `section_header`, using a heading_stack to maintain the parent chain.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Check whether `level` is consistent: same-level merging requires equal `level`, and cross-level absorption allows only shallow-absorbs-deep.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Long body text within a section may overlap**: multiple fragments split from one content line may keep R-style overlap by `chunk_overlap_token_size`, reducing mid-body cuts.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy uses `parent_headings` to preserve the hierarchical structure of the document, ensuring that the relationships between headings and subheadings are maintained during chunking.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `blocks_path` missing, unreadable, or with no valid content lines | Degrade wholesale to `chunking_by_recursive_character()`, passing the resolved `chunk_overlap_token_size` |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Rule**: **process from deeper levels to shallower levels** — first merge same-level small blocks (Phase A), then batch-absorb the tail, finally allow shallow blocks to absorb deep blocks (Phase B); every merge must simultaneously satisfy four constraints: size, table role, level, and parent-heading path.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Scenario | Overlaps? | Budget / mechanism | Effect served |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;→ §3.5 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE` / `LightRAG(chunk_overlap_token_size=…)` | `100` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. Compute the left budget `prev_budget = min(chunk_overlap_token_size, target_max - current token count of the left last slice)`.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Check whether a `middle` table slice is inserted in between: it blocks batched tail absorption.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;3. **Bridge text between tables may overlap bidirectionally**: the only cross-paragraph duplication scenario, dedicated to preserving context for consecutive large tables.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy considers `target_max` as a hard cap for chunk sizes and `target_ideal` as a soft target, ensuring chunks do not exceed a certain token limit while still being semantically meaningful.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| TableRowSplit cannot identify a table's JSON / HTML structure | That table uses R-strategy character splitting |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Effect**: many 100–300 token fine-grained clauses are merged toward near `target_ideal` (chunks are no longer too short and semantically thin), while **never lumping together adjacent small blocks that belong to different topics / different parent sections** — curing both &quot;chunks too small&quot; and &quot;cross-topic pollution&quot;.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Different `.blocks.jsonl` content lines (section boundaries) | **Never overlaps** | —— | Clear section boundaries, no mis-attribution |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;↓  LevelMerge: hierarchy-aware two-phase merging                                                   → §3.6 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For config syntax, the precedence chain, runtime overrides via `addon_params[&quot;chunker&quot;]`, etc., see [FileProcessingConfiguration-zh.md](FileProcessingConfiguration-zh.md) §3. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;3. Compute the right budget `next_budget = min(chunk_overlap_token_size, target_max - current token count of the right first slice)`.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Normally LevelMerge's real-token re-measurement rejects over-limit merges, but over-limit chunks can still appear in these scenarios:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;4. **Table rows never overlap each other**: row-level slicing is itself non-overlapping, distinct from R's overlap concept.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When dealing with tables, the P strategy ensures that tables under a certain size (`table_max`) remain whole, and large tables are sliced along row boundaries and re-wrapped as `&lt;table&gt;` elements.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| TableRowSplit finds a single row that cannot be kept within `target_max` alongside its header (the row content exceeds the cap, or it fits but the header would push it over) | **The whole table (header included) degrades to R-strategy character splitting and a `logger.warning` is logged**; the header content survives as plain text along with the original table text |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;1. **Size constraint**: the merged real text token count does not exceed `target_max`; a block that has reached `target_ideal` in principle no longer participates in ordinary same-level merging.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Long body text within one content line falling back to R | May overlap | `chunk_overlap_token_size` | Keeps semantic continuity at a mid-body cut |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;→ §3.6 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`P` is a chunking option orthogonal to the engine (`suffix:engine-options`) and can combine with any sidecar-producing engine. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;4. **If the bridge text fits within both side budgets**: both the left and right table boundary chunks contain the **complete bridge text**.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Tables not explicitly tagged but whose content can be sniffed as JSON / HTML are handled by the same rules.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A single-row table itself exceeds `target_max`, with no anchor to split on, ultimately going through R character splitting but a single chunk still exceeds the limit.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">13.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The table below maps each rule of §3 to the effect it achieves and the internal stage that implements it (stage names double as the cross-reference identifiers in code comments, log keywords, and debugging — see §7.6):">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy allows for overlap between chunks when a section is too long, ensuring that the text within one section can be split in a way that maintains readability and context.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| AnchorSplit finds a long block with no qualifying short-paragraph anchor | Table first → greedy packing → degrade to R character splitting if a single paragraph is too long |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Bridge text between consecutive large tables | Bidirectional overlap | `min(overlap, …, target_max/2)` per side | Bridge explanation serves as context for both the left and right tables |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Final chunk list |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A typical `LIGHTRAG_PARSER` setup enabling P: &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;5. **If the bridge text is longer**: the prefix enters the left last-slice chunk, the suffix enters the right first-slice chunk; the middle segment exceeding both budgets becomes a standalone ordinary text chunk. This middle chunk **keeps `chunk_overlap_token_size` of R-style overlap with each side**: extending left to re-include the tail of the prefix that went into the left table chunk, and right to include the head of the suffix that went into the right table chunk. Because each side's prefix/suffix is itself ≤ the overlap budget, the overlap span covers the entire prefix and suffix, so **the middle chunk in effect carries the complete bridge text** (the bridge is therefore never fragmented; only its head/tail are **additionally** copied into the neighbouring table chunks). The overlap indices always stay within the bridge tokens, so **`&lt;table&gt;` content is never copied into the middle chunk**.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **docling**: `label=&quot;title&quot;` → level 1, `label=&quot;section_header&quot;` → `item.level + 1` (default level 2), likewise maintaining the parent chain.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`enforce_chunk_token_limit_before_embedding` does a final hard split before embedding, so downstream never actually embeds an over-limit chunk into the vector store.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">13.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Chunking rule | Effect achieved | Implementing stage | See |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">24.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The target hard cap N, default `2000` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| HeaderRecovery finds `.tables.json` missing/unreadable, or the source table has no `table_header` | Skip header injection (that table has no repeating header to begin with; does not affect the rest of chunking) |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;3. **Level constraint**: same-level merging happens between equal `level`s; cross-level absorption allows only shallow-absorbs-deep, **forbidding deep from absorbing shallow in reverse**.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Standalone middle chunk of a long bridge | Overlaps each side | `chunk_overlap_token_size` (kept within bridge tokens, never includes `&lt;table&gt;`) | The middle reads continuously with the neighbouring table chunks |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Every rule of the P strategy serves one goal: align chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries, and make each chunk understandable on its own at recall time.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A single side's budget is further capped at no more than `chunk_token_size / 2`, so bridge text can never dominate the whole chunk.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;All three ultimately produce a unified `IRBlock` (carrying `heading` / `level` / `parent_headings`), persisted by `write_sidecar()` into an identically-structured `.blocks.jsonl`; tables, equations, and drawings are extracted as single-line tags (`&lt;table id=&quot;...&quot; format=&quot;json&quot;&gt;...&lt;/table&gt;` etc.) written to the corresponding sidecar.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;One original content line was split into multiple pieces but only one `[part 1]` is seen: check whether they were merged in LevelMerge — after merging the main block's part suffix is kept and not concatenated.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">13.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Heading-level basic chunks | Chunk boundaries align with the document's native structure, not token counts | HeadingBlocks | §3.1 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">24.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`chunk_overlap_token_size` | `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE` / `chunk_overlap_token_size` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Confirm the parser successfully produced `.blocks.jsonl`:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;4. **Parent-heading-path consistency constraint**: the key to avoiding cross-topic pollution, with strict semantics by merge direction —&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Between table row-level slices | **Never overlaps** | —— | Row slices are non-overlapping, avoiding duplicate rows |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;No matter how the rules combine, the following four overlap invariants always hold — they delimit 'where text duplication is allowed, and where it is never allowed':">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Many knowdge fragments with entities were missed in the last extraction. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;How this differs from ordinary adjacent chunk overlap:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Every recognizable heading triggers a basic-block boundary, with **no** token-threshold splitting.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A legacy `[表格片段N]` suffix appears: this means data output by an old chunker version; the new version unifies on `[part n]`, so re-chunk.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Table integrity + row-boundary slicing | Tables are not cut mid-cell; slices remain legal `&lt;table&gt;` | TableRowSplit | §3.2 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The cap on long-body fallback within one content line and on the table bridge budget, default `100` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If the file is missing or empty, the P strategy degrades wholesale to R and gains none of P's benefits. Common causes:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Same-level merging (Phase A / tail absorption)**: the two blocks' `parent_headings` must be **exactly equal** (true siblings). Blocks with the same `level` but different parent chains (e.g. `2.4.1` and `2.5.1`) may not merge.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Rule**: the P strategy's thresholds are not fixed constants but derived dynamically from `chunk_token_size` (denoted N); multiple thresholds act in concert to control the size of text chunks and table slices.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;1. Section boundaries never overlap: text between different `.blocks.jsonl` content lines is never copied into each other's chunks, avoiding mis-attribution.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The rowspan` / `colspan` merged-cell semantics, no longer expanding it into a span-less grid); if an HTML slice already carries a `&lt;thead&gt;` (the cut landed inside a multi-row header) injection is skipped to avoid duplication. The slice keeps its original `attrs` (including the leading `id`). Because the room was reserved, **a slice plus its header still stays `≤ target_max`**, so the hard cap is enforced naturally by every downstream stage — there is no late backfill that can overflow the cap. The first slice keeps its own real header row and is not injected again. If a slice has converged to a single row that can no longer hold both the row content and the header within `target_max` (see §3.2.2), **the whole table degrades to an R recursive character split (header included) and a warning is logged** — never leaving an orphaned header-less slice.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-524a118499dc3ffcc8a3626201ce9807</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Ordinary overlap copies characters or tokens in sequence, regardless of boundary type.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P chunker reads `.blocks.jsonl` directly, treating each content line as an independent processing unit for the subsequent TableRowSplit/AnchorSplit — which also means `[part n]` numbering is **reset independently** per original content line (see §3.4 and §4.4).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;P-strategy-related log keywords (for `grep` troubleshooting):&quot;\">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Table context gluing (roles + bidirectional bridge overlap) | A table's leading explanation, trailing commentary, and bridge text never detach from the table | TableRowSplit / TableBridge | §3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`tokenizer` | The tokenizer already resolved by LightRAG &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;An engine (`suffix:engine-options`) can combine with any sidecar-producing engine.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Cross-level absorption (Phase B, shallow-absorbs-deep)**: the deep block must be a **descendant** of the shallow one — the shallow block's full heading path (`parent_headings` + its own `heading`, with any `[part n]` stripped) must be a prefix of the deep block's `parent_headings`. A shallow block absorbing a deep block from a different branch is forbidden.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Effect**: under the ideal distribution, most chunks land in the `[target_ideal, target_max]` interval (about 1500–2000 tokens when N=2000); noticeably small chunks are usually just standalone-locked `middle` table slices or section-boundary tail blocks.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. Long body text within a section may overlap: multiple fragments split from one content line may keep R-style overlap by `chunk_overlap_token_size`, reducing mid-body cuts.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The first slice keeps its own real header row and is not injected again.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-524a118499dc3ffcc8a3626201ce9807</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- The TableBridge mechanism is triggered by table-slice roles, making the bridge text serve simultaneously as the left table's following context and the right table's preceding context, so a bridging explanation is not attributed to only one side's table nor scattered into a separate chunk that is hard to recall.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Rule**: a table whose token count does not exceed `table_max` **stays whole**; only a table exceeding `table_max` is sliced, and it is **sliced along row boundaries first** — the whole table degrades to character-level splitting only when a slice has collapsed to a single row that still cannot be expressed within the limit.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The following **stage names** are used as cross-reference identifiers in code comments, docstrings, logs, and tests. The &quot;Former name&quot; column gives the old letter scheme (which may still appear in historical commits / issues / PR discussions):&quot;\">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Header recovery (re-attach the header to middle/last slices at split time) | A split table's `middle`/`last` slices keep their column names when recalled alone, without ever exceeding the cap | HeaderRecovery | §3.3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The basis for all token counting and text-overlap extraction &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`LIGHTRAG_PARSER` setup enables P with specific format configurations for parsing.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Blocks with empty `parent_headings` (preamble / non-hierarchical input) are treated as path-compatible and allowed (no hierarchy to pollute).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Name | Formula | Value at N = 2000 | Technical meaning |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;3. Bridge text between tables may overlap bidirectionally: the only cross-paragraph duplication scenario, dedicated to preserving context for consecutive large tables.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If a slice has converged to a single row that can no longer hold both the row content and the header within `target_max` (see §3.2.2), **the whole table degrades to an R recursive character split (header included) and a warning is logged** — never leaving an orphaned header-less slice.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-524a118499dc3ffcc8a3626201ce9807</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;After a large table is sliced along row boundaries, the header row stays only in the **first slice**; `middle` / `last` slices thus lose the column names and cannot tell each column's meaning when recalled on their own. To fix this, **during TableRowSplit** the header row is re-injected into the non-first slices' own `&lt;table&gt;`, so every slice becomes a complete header-bearing table.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Effect**: a table is never cut in the &quot;middle of a cell&quot;; every slice is re-wrapped as a legal `&lt;table&gt;` tag, so downstream parsing and LLM reading can interpret it as a table rather than as broken markup fragments.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If it is `raw`, the legacy path was taken and P auto-degrades to R.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Anchor-driven long-block re-splitting | Over-long sections are split at semantic points, preserving heading levels | AnchorSplit | §3.4 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy **does not accept** `split_by_character` / `split_by_character_only`, because the normal path is driven by heading and paragraph structure.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Specific format configurations are used for different file formats in P parsing.&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;1. **Section boundaries never overlap**: text between different `.blocks.jsonl` content lines is never copied into each other's chunks, avoiding mis-attribution.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For adjacent blocks at the current level, when **the current block** is below `target_ideal`, the merged real token count is ≤ `target_max`, and the constraints above are satisfied, merge them into one block (the absorbed(&quot;hyper-relation&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `target_max` | N | 2000 | Text-chunk hard cap |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;4. Table rows never overlap each other: row-level slicing is itself non-overlapping, distinct from R's overlap concept.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Some sections have only a heading and no body of their own (heading-only), e.g.:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77b99487e9daf270364082281b0c65f7</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;1. **Header source**: at parse time each table's &quot;cross-page repeating header&quot; is written into the sibling `.tables.json` (entry field `table_header`; **only tables that genuinely carry a repeating header have this field**). **The field is stored in the table's own format so merged-cell semantics survive end-to-end**: a `format=&quot;json&quot;` table stores it as a JSON 2-D array string (e.g. `[[&quot;H1&quot;,&quot;H2&quot;]]`), a `format=&quot;html&quot;` table stores it as the raw `&lt;thead&gt;…&lt;/thead&gt;` fragment (preserving `rowspan` / `colspan`). P traces back to the matching table entry via the `id` preserved on the to-be-split `&lt;table&gt;` tag and takes its `table_header`.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- `format=&quot;json&quot;`: slice along the top-level JSON row array.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A table of unrecognized format can only go through character splitting and cannot start TableRowSplit's role mechanism.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Body-less heading gluing | A parent heading is never separated from its child content | HeadingGlue | §3.5 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;because the normal path is driven by heading and paragraph structure.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy parameters `CHUNK_P_SIZE` and `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE` are set to 2000 and 100 respectively.&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Is `full_docs[doc_id].parse_format` equal to `lightrag`? If it is `raw`, the legacy path was taken and P auto-degrades to R.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `target_ideal` | 0.75 × N | 1500 | Text-chunk ideal target; once reached, stops participating in ordinary same-level merging |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Table integrity + row-boundary slicing | Tables(&quot;hyper-relation&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A pre-pass (`_glue_heading_only_blocks`) is therefore inserted before LevelMerge. When the current block is heading-only (`content` consists solely of heading lines, detected by `^#{1,6} +`), it **glues forward only**:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77b99487e9daf270364082281b0c65f7</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;During TableRowSplit, the header row is re-injected into the non-first slices' own `&lt;table&gt;`, so every slice becomes a complete header-bearing table.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- `format=&quot;html&quot;`: slice along `&lt;tr&gt;...&lt;/tr&gt;` rows.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A table below the threshold stays whole and does not trigger first/middle/last slicing.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Hierarchy-aware merging | Fine-grained clauses are gathered toward the ideal size without cross-topic pollution | LevelMerge | §3.6 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy only processes `type == &quot;content&quot;` lines. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Option flags `i`, `t`, `e` denote image, table, and formula analysis respectively.&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Directional rules by table-slice role (all split-table slices are frozen; only `none` may merge):&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `table_max` | 0.625 × N | 1250 | Table slicing trigger threshold |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Paragraph Semantic Chunking Strategy targets documents with a clear sectional structure such as DOCX and PDF, aiming to align chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Trigger**: the immediately following block is **strictly deeper** (greater `level`) and its `table_chunk_role` is `none` or `first`.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77b99487e9daf270364082281b0c65f7</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `first` role is the first slice of the original table | Appended to the tail of the current accumulation block, so the table's **leading explanation** enters the same chunk as the first slice &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A bridge across content lines does not participate in TableBridge bidirectional overlap.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- If a row subset still exceeds `table_max` after re-wrapping, it is subdivided further within that row subset.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Overlap rules | Sufficient recall context, yet section/table boundaries are never mis-attributed | Throughout | §3.7 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Each content line typically contains: &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Chunking strategy `P` is combinable with different analysis types.&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Block role | Can absorb the next block forward | Can be absorbed by the previous block |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `table_ideal` | 0.375 × N | 750 | Table slice ideal size |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The core goal of the P strategy is to align chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries as much as possible, rather than deciding split points purely by token-length counting.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy prevents long-block re-splitting by considering table-row boundaries and heading levels, maintaining the structure of the document.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Hard cap preserved**: the sub-block came out of AnchorSplit within `target_max`, but prepending the parent heading line(s) can tip it over the cap. Since nothing downstream re-splits an over-limit block (LevelMerge only prevents it from growing further), an over-cap bonded block is re-split here: **first peel off the leading heading line(s)**, split the body at the **full `target_max`** (so later prefix-free body pieces keep the full budget), then glue the heading prefix back onto the **first body piece**. Only when the first body piece is too large to also hold the prefix is it alone re-split with a reduced cap — so a large prefix does not over-fragment the whole subsection. This way the heading always travels with real body content and is never sliced off as a heading-only orphan (which LevelMerge would otherwise absorb backward), and every emitted piece is still ≤ `target_max`. (Degenerate case: when the prefix alone fills the cap — a very long title, or a tiny `chunk_token_size` — it cannot be kept whole, so the whole block is split directly and the oversized heading line is character-split; here the cap wins over heading integrity.)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-77b99487e9daf270364082281b0c65f7</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `middle` role is a middle slice of the original table | Emitted standalone, avoiding merger with unrelated body text &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The parent-heading-path consistency constraint blocks cross-topic merging.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Size-threshold coordination | Most chunks land in `[target_ideal, target_max]` | Throughout | §3.8 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The parser guarantees &quot;the body under one heading as one basic block&quot; (native via heading-driven structural splitting, mineru / docling via their respective IR builders), with no token-threshold splitting at parse time. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The engine with `suffix:engine-options` can combine with any sidecar-producing engine, indicating compatibility with various parsing engines.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `none` | Yes | Yes |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `table_min_last` | 0.32 × `table_max` | 400 | Table last-slice swallow-back threshold (below this and mergeable → swallowed back into the previous slice) |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy mainly addresses four problems: Table context fracture, Underused hierarchy information, Imbalanced fine-grained section sizes, and Long-block re-splitting breaks structure.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `last` role is the last slice of the original table | Starts a fresh accumulation block, so the **trailing commentary** is automatically appended after the last slice &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- When a slice has converged to a single row that cannot be kept both `≤ target_max` and header-complete (the row's content itself exceeds the cap, or it fits but leaves no room for the header it would need), the whole table degrades to an R recursive character split of the original `&lt;table&gt;` text (whose body still carries the header), and a `logger.warning` is logged** — the header content survives as plain text along with the original table text and is never silently dropped, nor is a &quot;some `&lt;table&gt;` slices + some orphaned character fragments&quot; mixed output produced.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Same-level merging requires equal `level`, and cross-level absorption allows only shallow-absorbs-deep.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The rules above chain into a pipeline that takes `.blocks.jsonl` as input (**each `type == &quot;content&quot;` line is treated as one heading-level basic block**):">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Tables stay whole, inserted into `content` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Trigger | Degradation behaviour |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `LIGHTRAG_PARSER` setup is a configuration that enables the P parsing strategy, which is used for document parsing.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `first` | No | No |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `small_tail_threshold` | 0.125 × N | 250 | Tail-fragment absorption threshold |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy is valid for the structured output of any parser that can produce a `.blocks.jsonl` sidecar, such as `native` / `mineru` / `docling`.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The engine (`suffix:engine-options`) and can combine with any sidecar-producing engine.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A non-table slice or an unsplit whole table | Handled as an ordinary text chunk &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">12.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- A slice that needs no injected header and whose single row fits `target_max` is still kept whole as legal `&lt;table&gt;` markup.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It blocks batched tail absorption.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;DOCX / PDF / PPTX / …  ↓  native(docx) / mineru / docling parser —— emit basic blocks by heading, no token splitting |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Each element: &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The configuration specifies the use of native, mineru, and docling parsers for different file formats, with P as the common chunking strategy.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `middle` | No | No |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `max_anchor_candidate_length` | Fixed | 100 chars | Upper bound on candidate anchor-paragraph length for long-block splitting |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The R strategy is automatically degraded to when there is no sidecar or parse failure, while V strategy auto-degrades to R when there is no embedding model.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A typical `LIGHTRAG_PARSER` setup enabling P:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`table_chunk_role` is an internal field that does not survive into the final output, but it continues to serve as a merging constraint in LevelMerge (see §3.6.1).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;initiates tail absorption either.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;.blocks.jsonl + sidecar (.tables.json / .equations.json / .drawings.json / .blocks.assets/)  ↓  TableRowSplit: slice oversized tables along row boundaries and assign first/middle/last roles   → §3.2 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Optional: present only when the input .blocks.jsonl line carries a blockid, for the multimodal pipeline and document deletion to trace back by source block. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The header row is re-injected back at the top of their own `&lt;table&gt;`, so they remain interpretable per-column when recalled alone.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;` to recover the effective `&lt;w:outlineLvl&gt;`; walks the `document.xml` paragraphs resolving the outline level along the chain, mapping original outline levels 0–8 to internal `level` 1–9; maintains a `current_heading_stack`, clearing old headings no shallower than the current level and computing `parent_headings` on each new heading.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `last` | No | No |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Proportional constraints: `table_max &lt; target_ideal &lt; target_max`, `table_ideal &lt; table_max`. These ratios come from audit-mode empirical values (`large block 8000, small table 5000, ideal table 3000, table tail block 1600`) and are now scaled proportionally by `chunk_token_size`.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy uses heading outline level and `parent_headings` + table-row boundaries + anchors + hierarchy-aware merging for chunking.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`# docx uses native, pdf uses mineru, other supported formats use docling, all with P; unsupported formats fall back to legacy-R`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The bridge text is distributed bidirectionally by context budget &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- If a table's last slice has a token count below `table_min_last` and merging it with the previous slice does not exceed `table_max`, the last slice is swallowed back into the previous slice, reducing useless short table chunks.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For small blocks still unsaturated after Phase A, attempt cross-level merging, but allow only shallow-absorbs-deep:">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;→ §3.2 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Internally the implementation also uses temporary fields like `paragraphs`, `content`, `table_chunk_role`, `blockids` to aid splitting and merging, but they do **not** enter the final output under those names (`blockids` is materialized as `sidecar` after conversion). &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Each table slice is given an internal field `table_chunk_role`, and its role determines how it glues to surrounding paragraphs:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If an **ordinary (`none`)** block that has reached `target_ideal` is immediately followed by a run of same-level small blocks whose total token count is below `small_tail_threshold` and whose merged real token count does not exceed `target_max`, then **absorb that run in one shot**. Stop on encountering **any split-table slice** (`first` / `middle` / `last`), or when the parent-heading path diverges; a split-table slice never initiates tail absorption either.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Because merging inserts a newline joiner, summing per-block token counts may underestimate the merged result. **Before committing every merge, recompute the token count on the joined real text** and confirm it does not exceed `target_max` before committing.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy avoids cross-topic pollution by fully exploiting heading levels and table-row boundaries, and chunk boundaries hug semantics most closely.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`CHUNK_P_SIZE=2000`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The prefix enters the left last-slice chunk, the suffix enters the right first-slice chunk &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Rule**: a sliced table glues to surrounding paragraphs differently by &quot;first/middle/last&quot; role; short bridge text between two consecutive large tables is distributed **bidirectionally** to the table chunks on both sides by budget; middle/last slices that lose the header row get the table's repeating header re-injected into their own `&lt;table&gt; **during the split** (the header's tokens are budgeted out of each slice's cap before splitting).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- When the current block is shallower than the next block, the current block may absorb the next block forward.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;↓  HeaderRecovery: budget + re-inject the repeating header into middle/last slices during the split → §3.3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When one original `.blocks.jsonl` content line is split into multiple fragments, every fragment's `heading` field gets `[part 1]`, `[part 2]`, … &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Role | Meaning | Gluing strategy |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Check in this order: Does `full_docs[doc_id].process_options` include `P`?&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- After merging, the main block's `heading` is kept. If multiple part fragments are merged, the final heading keeps the main block's part suffix and does **not** additionally concatenate multiple part tags.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy has four overlap invariants: No overlap at section boundaries, Long body text overlaps by `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE`, Bridge text between consecutive large tables overlaps bidirectionally, and Heading metadata is inherited or promoted.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE=100`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The overlap indices always stay within the bridge tokens &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Effect**: a table's **leading explanation** enters the first-slice chunk, its **trailing commentary** enters the last-slice chunk, and **bridge text** serves as both the left table's following context and the right table's preceding context — any table slice carries enough context to be understood on its own at recall, with no &quot;table here, explanation in another chunk&quot; fracture. A split table's middle/last slices, even though detached from the first slice that carries the header, get the header row re-injected back at the top of their own `&lt;table&gt;`, so they remain interpretable per-column when recalled alone.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- When the current block is deeper than the previous block, the previous shallower block may absorb the current block.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;→ §3.3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;A content line that was not split keeps its original heading. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `first` | The first slice of the original table | Appended to the tail of the current accumulation block, so the table's **leading explanation** enters the same chunk as the first slice |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Does the `.blocks.jsonl` pointed to by `lightrag_document_path` exist and is it non-empty?&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Different `.blocks.jsonl` content lines (section boundaries) | **Never overlaps** | —— | Clear section boundaries, no mis-attribution |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy has design goals: Align chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries, Make each chunk understandable on its own at recall time, and Decompose this goal into concrete rules for three scenarios (tables, long blocks, fine-grained sections).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The option flags `i`/`t`/`e` mean image/table/formula analysis respectively, and `P` is the chunking strategy, combinable as needed.&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The slice keeps its original `attrs` (including the leading `id`). &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Each table slice is given an internal field `table_chunk_role`, and its role determines how it glues to surrounding paragraphs:">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Merging in the reverse direction is forbidden.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;↓  TableBridge: bidirectional overlap of bridge text between consecutive large tables              → §3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`parent_headings` gets no suffix. &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `middle` | A middle slice of the original table | Emitted standalone, avoiding merger with unrelated body text |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Are there `paragraph_semantic ... fallback to recursive_character` lines in the log?&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Long body text within one content line falling back to R | May overlap | `chunk_overlap_token_size` | Keeps semantic continuity at a mid-body cut |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy targets documents with a clear sectional structure, aligning chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries as much as possible.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Or override per file:&quot;&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The header's token cost is reserved out of each slice's body cap before splitting &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Role | Meaning | Gluing strategy |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Split-table slices (`first` / `middle` / `last`) are likewise frozen in the cross-level stage and do not participate; only `none` blocks take part in cross-level absorption.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;→ §3.3 |">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The numbering is **reset independently** within each original content line (because PartLabeling numbers before cross-line merging, see §2.2). &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `last` | The last slice of the original table | Starts a fresh accumulation block, so the **trailing commentary** is automatically appended after the last slice |&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Check whether the table was actually identified as `&lt;table format=&quot;json&quot;&gt;` or `&lt;table format=&quot;html&quot;&gt;` (look at `.blocks.jsonl`). A table of unrecognized format can only go through character splitting and cannot start TableRowSplit's role mechanism.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-1f6e0884f12ea67e008e26e0216f0a4e</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Every rule of the P strategy serves one goal: **align chunk boundaries with the document's native semantic boundaries, and make each chunk understandable on its own at recall time**.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy aims to address the problem of table context fracture after a large table is split, ensuring the head/tail slices remain connected to their explanation or commentary.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`my-proposal.[native-P].docx`&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The header row is re-injected into the non-first slices' own `&lt;table&gt;` &quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-0a78f97cbe007105c80acba2db42453d</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `first` | The first slice of the original table | App(&quot;hyper-relation&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">5.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Because merging inserts a newline joiner, summing per-block token counts may underestimate the merged result. **Before committing every merge, recompute the token count on the joined real text** and confirm it does not exceed `target_max` before committing.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-b84c6dc4e56f32ef79bfc21440cf2ef4</data>
</node>
