  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To cover the basic use cases for GraphRAG query, you should have two or three tables derived from your data.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- relationships.parquet - this is the list of relationships found in the dataset, which are the edges of the graph.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To cover the basic use cases for GraphRAG query, you should have two or three tables derived from your data: - entities.parquet - this is the list of entities found in the dataset, which are the nodes of the graph. - relationships.parquet - this is the list of relationships found in the dataset, which are the edges of the graph. - text_units.parquet - this is the source text chunks the graph was extracted from. This is optional depending on the query method you intend to use (described later).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- text_units.parquet - this is the source text chunks the graph was extracted from. This is optional depending on the query method you intend to use (described later).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- entities.parquet - this is the list of entities found in the dataset, which are the nodes of the graph.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The approach described here will be to run a custom GraphRAG workflow pipeline that assumes the text chunking, entity extraction, and relationship extraction has already occurred.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">16.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Several users have asked if they can bring their own existing graph and have it summarized for query with GraphRAG.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;See the full relationships [table schema](./outputs.md#relationships). For graph summarization purposes, you only need id, source, target, description, weight, and the list of text_unit_ids.">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">8.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;There are many possible ways to do this, but here we'll describe a simple method that aligns with the existing GraphRAG workflows quite easily.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</node>
