  <data key="d2">chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;RAG techniques have shown promise in helping LLMs to reason about _private datasets_ - data that the LLM is not trained on and has never seen before, such as an enterprise’s proprietary research, business documents, or communications. _Baseline RAG_ was created to help solve this problem, but we observe situations where baseline RAG performs very poorly.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Please see the [breaking changes](https://github.com/microsoft/graphrag/blob/main/breaking-changes.md) document for notes on our approach to versioning the project.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">15.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;GraphRAG is a structured, hierarchical approach to Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG), as opposed to naive semantic-search approaches using plain text snippets.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">18.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Use the migration notebook to avoid re-indexing prior datasets between major version bumps.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;At query time, these structures are used to provide materials for the LLM context window when answering a question. The primary query modes are:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Run the provided migration notebook between major version bumps if you want to avoid re-indexing prior datasets. Note that this will overwrite your configuration and prompts, so backup if necessary.*&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To address this, the tech community is working to develop methods that extend and enhance RAG. Microsoft Research’s new approach, GraphRAG, creates a knowledge graph based on an input corpus. This graph, along with community summaries and graph machine learning outputs, are used to augment prompts at query time. GraphRAG shows substantial improvement in answering the two classes of questions described above, demonstrating intelligence or mastery that outperforms other approaches previously applied to private datasets.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;*Always run `graphrag init --root [path] --force` between minor version bumps to ensure you have the latest config format. Run the provided migration notebook between major version bumps if you want to avoid re-indexing prior datasets. Note that this will overwrite your configuration and prompts, so backup&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The GraphRAG process involves extracting a knowledge graph out of raw text, building a community hierarchy, generating summaries for these communities, and then leveraging these structures when performing RAG-based tasks.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">18.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Backup your configuration and prompts before running the migration notebook if they will be overwritten.*&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- [_Global Search_](query/global_search.md) for reasoning about holistic questions about the corpus by leveraging the community summaries.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Backup if necessary.*&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">6.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-6c57397a9af89823fd938ed732f91884</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;GraphRAG builds upon our prior [research](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/patterns-hidden-inside-the-org-chart) and [tooling](https://github.com/graspologic-org/graspologic) using graph machine learning. The basic steps of the GraphRAG process are as follows:&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">9.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;👉 [Microsoft Research Blog Post](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/graphrag-unlocking-llm-discovery-on-narrative-private-data/)&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To learn more about GraphRAG and how it can be used to enhance your language model's ability to reason about your private data, please visit the [Microsoft Research Blog Post](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/graphrag-unlocking-llm-discovery-on-narrative-private-data/).&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">14.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
<node id="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- [_Local Search_](query/local_search.md) for reasoning about specific entities by fanning-out to their neighbors and associated concepts.&quot;">
  <data key="d0">hyperedge</data>
  <data key="d1">7.0</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-71f126a1f713fce89f72bd739d4c81eb</data>
</node>
