</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;whenever it changes.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-100681c982044751140d02d193a76196</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`uv.lock` is uv's lock file. It captures the exact version of every dependency, including transitive ones, much like: - Node.js `package-lock.json` - Rust `Cargo.lock` - Python Poetry `poetry.lock`&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-56d43eaedfdb0bd6c70bdaa2381e96ea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Keeping `uv.lock` in version control guarantees that everyone installs the same dependency set.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-56d43eaedfdb0bd6c70bdaa2381e96ea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Should we commit `uv.lock`?** - Yes. Commit it so collaborators and CI jobs share the same dependency graph.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-56d43eaedfdb0bd6c70bdaa2381e96ea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **Can `uv.lock` and `requirements.txt` coexist?** - They can, but maintaining both is redundant. Prefer relying on `uv.lock` alone whenever possible.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-56d43eaedfdb0bd6c70bdaa2381e96ea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UV.LOCK&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **How do I inspect locked versions?** - ```bash```uv tree```grep -A5 'name = &quot;openai&quot;' uv.lock```&quot;">
