</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Configuration settings for MINERU and DOCLING are specified.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3219553e6504cf126a5429743398bc71</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- The `native` / `mineru` / `docling` paths first enqueue with `pending_parse`; after parsing completes and `content_hash` is filled in, if another document already has the same hash, this record is stopped before entering analysis, chunking, entity extraction, and graph writing.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-02c5bff8f2fe6a621cc9a6ced39ccfaf</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Supports multiple file content extraction engines: legacy, native, mineru, docling.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">60.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-502fd83a7f153d429f3536ae34c05bb2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;```text\npaper.[mineru(page_range=1-3,language=en,local_parse_method=ocr)].pdf   # filename hint\nscan.[docling(force_ocr=true)].pdf\nLIGHTRAG_PARSER=pdf:mineru(language=en);*:legacy-R                       # rule\n```&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Currently supported engine parameters (canonical / alias):&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- Only `mineru` and `docling` accept engine parameters; attaching one to `legacy`/`native` is a friendly error. Validation runs at startup (`LIGHTRAG_PARSER`) and at upload.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- `parse_engine` is stored in hint syntax (e.g. `mineru(page_range=1-3)`) and shown in `doc_status` metadata so you can see the parse parameters a document used.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| Engine | Description | Supported file formats (extensions) |&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`mineru` and `docling` are external content extraction engines; before enabling related rules, the services must be running first, and the corresponding endpoint/token must be configured in LightRAG.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04c25eb38b88488839f25538326154e2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;parameters of the `mineru` / `docling` engines will also invalidate the cache, causing the engine to re-parse the file content on the next upload of the same file.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-3823ede94fd87dec38980f3c5e83b533</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Software packages requiring `transformers`, `torch`, or `cuda` will not be included in the offline dependency group. Consequently, document extraction tools such as Docling, as well as local LLM models like Hugging Face and LMDeploy, are outside the scope of offline installation support. These high-compute-resource-demanding services should not be integrated into LightRAG. Docling will be decoupled and deployed as a standalone service.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-282ce1c61fc13efc3e86b9d5f4e51547</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- **docling**: `label=&quot;title&quot;` → level 1, `label=&quot;section_header&quot;` → `item.level + 1` (default level 2), likewise maintaining the parent chain.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">75.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-631139d87a5854bded80f7eef24fed9c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The P strategy is valid for the structured output of any parser that can produce a `.blocks.jsonl` sidecar, such as `native` / `mineru` / `docling`.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The parser guarantees &quot;the body under one heading as one basic block&quot; (native via heading-driven structural splitting, mineru / docling via their respective IR builders), with no token-threshold splitting at parse time. &quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCLING&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`# docx uses native, pdf uses mineru, other supported formats use docling, all with P; unsupported formats fall back to legacy-R`&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LMDEPLOY&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Software packages requiring `transformers`, `torch`, or `cuda` will not be included in the offline dependency group. Consequently, document extraction tools such as Docling, as well as local LLM models like Hugging Face and LMDeploy, are outside the scope of offline installation support. These high-compute-resource-demanding services should not be integrated into LightRAG. Docling will be decoupled and deployed as a standalone service.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-282ce1c61fc13efc3e86b9d5f4e51547</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LMDEPLOY&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG uses dynamic package installation (`pipmaster`) for optional features based on file types and configurations. In offline environments, these dynamic installations will fail. This guide shows you how to pre-install all necessary dependencies and cache files.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">170.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-282ce1c61fc13efc3e86b9d5f4e51547</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ENVIRONMENT&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The configuration options for LightRAG include setting up the environment for the service to function properly. This involves initializing the necessary parameters and configurations before starting the service. &quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER CONFIGURATION&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For a first run, follow Progressive Setup Recipes instead of copying the entire env.example file by hand.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SERVER CONFIGURATION&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The examples below are reference snippets for tuning existing deployments. For a first run, follow [Progressive Setup Recipes](#progressive-setup-recipes) instead of copying the entire `env.example` file by hand.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVICE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The Docker compose command is used to start the LightRAG service in a local environment. This command ensures that all necessary components are up and running. &quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The setup wizard helps you configure LightRAG in three parts: `env-base` sets up the LLM, embedding model, and optional reranker; `env-storage` adds or changes storage backends such as PostgreSQL, Neo4j, Redis, Milvus, Qdrant, MongoDB, or Memgraph; `env-server` sets server host and port, WebUI labels, authentication, API keys, and SSL.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1d78cb23a1beadc3e7c5c84bc0595ed1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;3-large is a document that includes several configuration options for a service called LightRAG. The configuration is detailed in various steps that pertain to different aspects of the service, including embedding dimensions, data storage, reranking, and deployment settings. This text seems to be a reference document for setting up and operating LightRAG.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Changing the embedding model, embedding dimension, asymmetric embedding behavior, or query/document prefixes changes vector semantics. Clear the affected LightRAG workspace/vector data and re-index source files.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG necessitates the integration of both an LLM (Large Language Model) and an Embedding Model to effectively execute document indexing and querying operations. Prior to the initial deployment of the LightRAG server, it is essential to configure the settings for both the LLM and the Embedding Model.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Important Note: The embedding model and asymmetric embedding configuration must be determined before document indexing, and the same settings must be used during the query phase. For certain storage solutions (e.g., PostgreSQL), the vector dimension must be defined upon initial table creation. When changing the embedding model, embedding dimension, `EMBEDDING_ASYMMETRIC`, query/document prefixes, or provider task behavior, clear the existing LightRAG workspace/vector data and re-index the source files.">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The embedding model and asymmetric embedding configuration must be decided before document indexing and must remain consistent during the query phase.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Important Note**: The embedding model and asymmetric embedding configuration must be determined before document indexing, and the same settings must be used during the query phase. For certain storage solutions (e.g., PostgreSQL), the vector dimension must be defined upon initial table creation. When changing the embedding model, embedding dimension, `EMBEDDING_ASYMMETRIC`, query/document prefixes, or provider task behavior, clear the existing LightRAG workspace/vector data and re-index the source files.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Changing the embedding model, embedding dimension, asymmetric embedding behavior, or query/document prefixes changes vector semantics. Clear the affected LightRAG workspace/vector data and re-index source files.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;EMBEDDING MODEL&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG necessitates the integration of both an LLM (Large Language Model) and an Embedding Model to effectively execute document indexing and querying operations.">
