<edge source="&quot;DOCKER IMAGE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;After preparing the two files above, build the Docker image with the following command: &quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-329eac639a865519f1557ce9dc303a63</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CUDA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Software packages requiring `transformers`, `torch`, or `cuda` will not be included in the offline dependency group. Consequently, document extraction tools such as Docling, as well as local LLM models like Hugging Face and LMDeploy, are outside the scope of offline installation support. These high-compute-resource-demanding services should not be integrated into LightRAG. Docling will be decoupled and deployed as a standalone service.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-282ce1c61fc13efc3e86b9d5f4e51547</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;--PORT 8000&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The command executed for mineru-api includes --port 8000 &quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8cedc79909a2121d28e3444802703cf3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;8000:8000&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Port 8000 is exposed for mineru-api &quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8cedc79909a2121d28e3444802703cf3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL DEVELOPMENT&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The text describes how to set up local development with `bun run dev` and the importance of accessing the root path (`http://localhost:5173/`) regardless of the scenario.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8695c3b8cfc1cc28382a176ac4d0e7ba</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LOCAL DEVELOPMENT&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `uv sync`             | ⚠️ Maybe          | Local development; can regenerate the lock |&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-100681c982044751140d02d193a76196</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`MAX_PARALLEL_INSERT` controls how many files are processed in parallel. `MAX_ASYNC_LLM` (deprecated alias: `MAX_ASYNC`) controls concurrent LLM calls, including extraction, merging, query keyword generation, and final answer generation. Optional staged-pipeline variables such as `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_NATIVE`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_DOCLING`, and `MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE` can be used for parser-heavy deployments.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM falls back to MAX_ASYNC_LLM when unset (MAX_ASYNC is still accepted as a deprecated alias)&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM=4&quot;">
  <data key="d5">160.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To maintain compatibility with legacy data, the default workspace for PostgreSQL is `default` and for Neo4j is `base` when no workspace is configured. For all external storages, the system provides dedicated workspace environment variables to override the common `WORKSPACE` environment variable configuration. These storage-specific workspace environment variables are: `REDIS_WORKSPACE`, `MILVUS_WORKSPACE`, `QDRANT_WORKSPACE`, `MONGODB_WORKSPACE`, `POSTGRES_WORKSPACE`, `NEO4J_WORKSPACE`, `MEMGRAPH_WORKSPACE`, `OPENSEARCH_WORKSPACE.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;On macOS, Gunicorn multi-worker mode also requires the Objective-C fork-safety override to be present before the Python process starts. Do not rely on `.env` for this variable; `.env` is loaded after Python startup and is too late for the Objective-C runtime: export OBJC_DISABLE_INITIALIZE_FORK_SAFETY=YES lightrag-gunicorn --workers 2 &quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`MAX_PARALLEL_INSERT` controls how many files are processed in parallel. `MAX_ASYNC_LLM` (deprecated alias: `MAX_ASYNC`) controls concurrent LLM calls, including extraction, merging, query keyword generation, and final answer generation.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Below are several environment variables related to concurrent processing in LightRAG Server, including 'WORKERS', 'MAX_PARALLEL_INSERT', and 'MAX_ASYNC_LLM'.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_ASYNC_LLM&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The base configuration includes the LLM provider, model name, service endpoint, API key, timeout, and concurrency settings.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-082eb07a755d44999510e8f240806102</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SETUP WIZARD&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The setup wizard can be used to generate a `.env` file with configuration options for the LightRAG Server.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
