<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The LightRAG Server is designed to provide a Web UI and API support. The Web UI facilitates document indexing, knowledge graph exploration, and a simple RAG query interface. LightRAG Server also provides an Ollama-compatible interface, aiming to emulate LightRAG as an Ollama chat model. This allows AI chat bots, such as Open WebUI, to access LightRAG easily.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">95.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;* Install from PyPI&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is recommended to use environment variables to configure the LightRAG Server.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For a full description of every target and what each flow does, see [docs/InteractiveSetup.md](./InteractiveSetup.md).&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When using Nginx as a reverse proxy in front of LightRAG Server, you need to configure `client_max_body_size` for the `/documents/upload` endpoint to handle large file uploads.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">180.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;After modifying the `.env` file, you need to reopen the terminal for the new settings to take effect. This is because each time LightRAG Server starts, it loads the environment variables from the `.env` file into the system environment variables, and system environment variables have higher precedence.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d7149569405d52b329c2d9c690f051dd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;```shell docker compose up ``` # If you want the program to run in the background after startup, add the -d parameter at the end of the command.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d7149569405d52b329c2d9c690f051dd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;This is because each time LightRAG Server starts, it loads the environment variables from the `.env` file into the system environment variables, and system environment variables have higher precedence.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d7149569405d52b329c2d9c690f051dd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;By default, the LightRAG Server can be accessed without any authentication. We can configure the server with an API Key or account credentials to secure it.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-e07113c82d8f800d43e240dceb9a4230</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is recommended to use environment variables to configure the LightRAG Server. There is an example environment variable file named `env.example` in the root directory of the project.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The setup wizards update configuration only; run `make env-security-check` separately to audit the current `.env` for security risks before deployment.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is recommended to use environment variables to configure the LightRAG Server. There is an example environment variable file named `env.example` in the root directory of the project. Please copy this file to the startup directory and rename it to `.env`. After that, you can modify the parameters related to the LLM and Embedding models in the `.env` file. It is important to note that the LightRAG Server will load the environment variables from `.env` into the system environment variables each time it starts. **LightRAG Server will prioritize the settings in the system environment variables to .env file**.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The LightRAG Server is designed to provide a Web UI and API support.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-8286dc1e92acbe46895a3e4b4adbe05d</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The LightRAG Server can operate in the 'Gunicorn + Uvicorn' preload mode to prevent document indexing tasks from blocking RAG queries.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;If you want to integrate LightRAG into your project, we recommend using the REST API provided by the LightRAG Server. LightRAG Core is intended for embedded applications or researchers conducting studies and evaluations.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-28550335f56f105359c1f2c45cf572d1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG SERVER&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG Core is intended for embedded applications or researchers conducting studies and evaluations.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-28550335f56f105359c1f2c45cf572d1</data>
</edge>
