<edge source="&quot;ENV.EXAMPLE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is recommended to use environment variables to configure the LightRAG Server. There is an example environment variable file named `env.example` in the root directory of the project.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ENV.EXAMPLE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It is recommended to use environment variables to configure the LightRAG Server. There is an example environment variable file named `env.example` in the root directory of the project. Please copy this file to the startup directory and rename it to `.env`. After that, you can modify the parameters related to the LLM and Embedding models in the `.env` file. It is important to note that the LightRAG Server will load the environment variables from `.env` into the system environment variables each time it starts. **LightRAG Server will prioritize the settings in the system environment variables to .env file**.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ENV.EXAMPLE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For comprehensive reranker configuration examples, including Cohere-compatible chunking options and Jina/Aliyun endpoints, refer to the `env.example` file.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-58732a4b6a97c3eeba3cb6d1206d401e</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;ENV.EXAMPLE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;For LLM and embedding configuration examples, please refer to the `env.example` file in the project's root directory:">
  <data key="d5">160.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-0a5f058377b951c53da07f784f3944ef</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`MAX_PARALLEL_INSERT` controls how many files are processed in parallel. `MAX_ASYNC_LLM` (deprecated alias: `MAX_ASYNC`) controls concurrent LLM calls, including extraction, merging, query keyword generation, and final answer generation. Optional staged-pipeline variables such as `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_NATIVE`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_DOCLING`, and `MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE` can be used for parser-heavy deployments.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Optional staged-pipeline variables such as `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_NATIVE`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_DOCLING`, and `MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE` can be used for parser-heavy deployments.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DEFAULT_CHUNK_P_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_P_SIZE` | `2000` (uses `DEFAULT_CHUNK_P_SIZE` when unset; does **not** inherit `CHUNK_SIZE`) &quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;`MAX_PARALLEL_INSERT` controls how many files are processed in parallel. `MAX_ASYNC_LLM` (deprecated alias: `MAX_ASYNC`) controls concurrent LLM calls, including extraction, merging, query keyword generation, and final answer generation. Optional staged-pipeline variables such as `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_NATIVE`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_DOCLING`, and `MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE` can be used for parser-heavy deployments.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Optional staged-pipeline variables such as `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_NATIVE`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_MINERU`, `MAX_PARALLEL_PARSE_DOCLING`, and `MAX_PARALLEL_ANALYZE` can be used for parser-heavy deployments.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCKERFILE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Browsing `https://host.example.com/site01/webui/` shows site01's WebUI; `https://host.example.com/site02/webui/` shows site02's. The same Docker image serves both — no per-site build artifact, no rebuild on prefix changes.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1ff6c169abcd9da4c26e2eca92a0f7ad</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCKERFILE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Keeping `uv.lock` in version control guarantees that everyone installs the same dependency set.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-56d43eaedfdb0bd6c70bdaa2381e96ea</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCKERFILE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The guide includes instructions for using `uv.lock` within a Docker environment.">
