<edge source="&quot;CHUNK_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Chunker parameters are configured with `CHUNK_SIZE`, `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE`, and strategy-specific variables such as `CHUNK_R_SEPARATORS`, `CHUNK_V_BREAKPOINT_THRESHOLD_TYPE`, `CHUNK_P_SIZE`, and `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE`.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">150.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CHUNK_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_P_SIZE` | `2000` (uses `DEFAULT_CHUNK_P_SIZE` when unset; does **not** inherit `CHUNK_SIZE`) &quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCUMENTS&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;All input formats are loaded within GraphRAG and passed to the indexing pipeline as a `documents` DataFrame. This DataFrame has a row for each document using a shared column schema:">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-813bfd09c5663aba506972a834914fb8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DOCUMENTS&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The default pipeline produces a series of output tables that align with the [conceptual knowledge model](../index/default_dataflow.md). This page describes the detailed output table schemas. By default we write these tables out as parquet files on disk.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f79b90a7af3598028d581d94c6d76678</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Chunker parameters are configured with `CHUNK_SIZE`, `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE`, and strategy-specific variables such as `CHUNK_R_SEPARATORS`, `CHUNK_V_BREAKPOINT_THRESHOLD_TYPE`, `CHUNK_P_SIZE`, and `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE`.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">150.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-84523726bee4688a44b0a4ac3253a74c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_P_OVERLAP_SIZE` | Unset (inherits `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE`) &quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| `CHUNK_OVERLAP_SIZE` / `LightRAG(chunk_overlap_token_size=…)` | `100` &quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f62767a155c127d2e19bb2a763d570c3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;UPLOAD&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The WebUI can be accessed after the service is started. Users can upload documents and run queries through the WebUI. &quot;">
  <data key="d5">70.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c19f3acc7d1050e16d5cc8de39fec3a4</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TABLES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;It decomposes this goal into concrete rules for three scenarios (tables, long blocks, fine-grained sections), expanded one by one in §3.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-dfdc14720aca7f1c8740b02182c2b4d8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TABLES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To cover the basic use cases for GraphRAG query, you should have two or three tables derived from your data.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;TABLES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To cover the basic use cases for GraphRAG query, you should have two or three tables derived from your data: - entities.parquet - this is the list of entities found in the dataset, which are the nodes of the graph. - relationships.parquet - this is the list of relationships found in the dataset, which are the edges of the graph. - text_units.parquet - this is the source text chunks the graph was extracted from. This is optional depending on the query method you intend to use (described later).&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The text describes the structure and content of a technical document, including metadata fields and their descriptions, which is relevant for document management and version control.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-6df28f5232d5c31b9f64164c61a08e65</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Run any number of containers from the same image, each with its own prefix:">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1ff6c169abcd9da4c26e2eca92a0f7ad</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Once a `BaseParser` subclass is implemented, the engine needs to be registered to be recognized and used by the LightRAG system. This registration is done through a `ParserSpec` object, which contains metadata about the engine such as its name, version, and supported file types. The entry point for automatic discovery of these `ParserSpec` objects is the `lightrag.parsers` directory, where third-party engines can provide their own modules that are discovered at runtime by LightRAG.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| metadata      | dict | Optional additional document metadata. More details below.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-813bfd09c5663aba506972a834914fb8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Also see the [outputs](outputs.md) documentation for the final documents table schema saved to parquet after pipeline completion.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-813bfd09c5663aba506972a834914fb8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;With the CSV format you can configure the `text_column`, and `title_column` if your data has structured content you would prefer to use. If you do not configure these within the `input` block of your settings.yaml, the title will be the filename as described in the schema above. The `text_column` is assumed to be &quot;text&quot; in your file if not configured specifically.">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-813bfd09c5663aba506972a834914fb8</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;METADATA&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The default pipeline produces a series of output tables that align with the [conceptual knowledge model](../index/default_dataflow.md). This page describes the detailed output table schemas. By default we write these tables out as parquet files on disk.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-f79b90a7af3598028d581d94c6d76678</data>
</edge>
