<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Different instances access the same database instance, you need to use workspaces to achieve data isolation; otherwise, the data of different instances will conflict and be destroyed.&quot;" target="&quot;DATABASE INSTANCE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Different instances access the same database instance, you need to use workspaces to achieve data isolation; otherwise, the data of different instances will conflict and be destroyed.&quot;" target="&quot;WORKSPACES&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Different instances access the same database instance, you need to use workspaces to achieve data isolation; otherwise, the data of different instances will conflict and be destroyed.&quot;" target="&quot;DATA ISOLATION&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The command-line `workspace` argument and the `WORKSPACE` environment variable in the `.env` file can both be used to specify the workspace name for the current instance, with the command-line argument having higher priority.&quot;" target="&quot;COMMAND-LINE `WORKSPACE` ARGUMENT&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The command-line `workspace` argument and the `WORKSPACE` environment variable in the `.env` file can both be used to specify the workspace name for the current instance, with the command-line argument having higher priority.&quot;" target="&quot;WORKSPACE ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Here is how workspaces are implemented for different types of storage: for local file-based databases, data isolation is achieved through workspace subdirectories; for databases that store data in collections, it's done by adding a workspace prefix to the collection name; for Qdrant vector database, data isolation is achieved through payload-based partitioning (Qdrant's recommended multitenancy approach); for relational databases, data isolation is achieved by adding a `workspace` field to the tables for logical data separation; for graph databases, logical data isolation is achieved through labels; for OpenSearch, data isolation is achieved through index name prefixes.&quot;" target="&quot;LOCAL FILE-BASED DATABASES&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Here is how workspaces are implemented for different types of storage: for local file-based databases, data isolation is achieved through workspace subdirectories; for databases that store data in collections, it's done by adding a workspace prefix to the collection name; for Qdrant vector database, data isolation is achieved through payload-based partitioning (Qdrant's recommended multitenancy approach); for relational databases, data isolation is achieved by adding a `workspace` field to the tables for logical data separation; for graph databases, logical data isolation is achieved through labels; for OpenSearch, data isolation is achieved through index name prefixes.&quot;" target="&quot;JSONKVSTORAGE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Here is how workspaces are implemented for different types of storage: for local file-based databases, data isolation is achieved through workspace subdirectories; for databases that store data in collections, it's done by adding a workspace prefix to the collection name; for Qdrant vector database, data isolation is achieved through payload-based partitioning (Qdrant's recommended multitenancy approach); for relational databases, data isolation is achieved by adding a `workspace` field to the tables for logical data separation; for graph databases, logical data isolation is achieved through labels; for OpenSearch, data isolation is achieved through index name prefixes.&quot;" target="&quot;NETWORKXSTORAGE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-1417f2fecf74afcbf4d69be846fd679a</data>
</edge>
