<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG INSTANCES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;LightRAG instances are launched simultaneously with different `.env` files to configure them individually.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d7149569405d52b329c2d9c690f051dd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG INSTANCES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;There are two ways to start multiple LightRAG instances. The first way is to configure a completely independent working environment for each instance. This requires creating a separate working directory for each instance and placing a dedicated `.env` configuration file in that directory. The server listening ports in the configuration files of different instances cannot be the same. Then, you can start the service by running `lightrag-server` in the working directory.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-9a514646a651da9026d7f8eb73aa2f08</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;LIGHTRAG INSTANCES&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;This document explains how to run multiple isolated LightRAG instances behind one host using a reverse proxy (nginx, Traefik, Kubernetes Ingress, …), with one shared WebUI build reused by every instance.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-900da8eeab3b8744fbf99eb48a223646</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;VECTOR DIMENSION&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;**Important Note**: The embedding model and asymmetric embedding configuration must be determined before document indexing, and the same settings must be used during the query phase. For certain storage solutions (e.g., PostgreSQL), the vector dimension must be defined upon initial table creation. When changing the embedding model, embedding dimension, `EMBEDDING_ASYMMETRIC`, query/document prefixes, or provider task behavior, clear the existing LightRAG workspace/vector data and re-index the source files.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-021f97620f79cb0c99aad301145dfec1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMMAND-LINE PARAMETERS&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;During startup, configurations in the `.env` file can be overridden by command-line parameters.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d7149569405d52b329c2d9c690f051dd</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;COMMAND-LINE PARAMETERS&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Command-line parameters can modify the configurations in the `.env` file during startup.&quot;">
