  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-c57c83519f50b4e9e94ec0fa78d3401a</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| AnchorSplit finds a long block with no qualifying short-paragraph anchor | Table first → greedy packing → degrade to R character splitting if a single paragraph is too long |&quot;" target="&quot;ANCHORSPLIT FINDS A LONG BLOCK WITH NO QUALIFYING SHORT-PARAGRAPH ANCHOR&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;| AnchorSplit finds a long block with no qualifying short-paragraph anchor | Table first → greedy packing → degrade to R character splitting if a single paragraph is too long |&quot;" target="&quot;TABLE FIRST GREEDY PACKING DEGRADE TO R CHARACTER SPLITTING IF A SINGLE PARAGRAPH IS TOO LONG&quot;">
  <data key="d5">80.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a157ba8bb77c0492f14d1d2322564184</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge." target="&quot;TABLEROWSPLIT&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge." target="&quot;MERGE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge." target="&quot;ROLE CONSTRAINT (SLICE FREEZE)&quot;">
  <data key="d5">90.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge." target="&quot;SPLIT-TABLE SLICE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;2. **Role constraint (slice freeze)**: every split-table slice — `first` / `middle` / `last` — is **locked standalone and never participates in any merge** (it neither absorbs forward, nor is absorbed backward, nor joins batched tail absorption). Reason: the repeating header is injected into each slice at TableRowSplit time, so re-merging two slices of one table would duplicate the header mid-body (§3.3.3). A table's boundary explanations were already glued into the first/last slices during the split, so the freeze does not lose context gluing — it only gives up the post-hoc consolidation of small first/last blocks with unrelated neighbours. Only `none` (an ordinary block / an unsplit whole table) may merge." target="&quot;LOCKED STANDALONE&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-d5becb2ed078b428ad8287c1f5c91acf</data>
</edge>
