<edge source="&quot;DATASET&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;- entities.parquet - this is the list of entities found in the dataset, which are the nodes of the graph.&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;DATASET&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;To cover the basic use cases for GraphRAG query, you should have two or three tables derived from your data: - entities.parquet - this is the list of entities found in the dataset, which are the nodes of the graph. - relationships.parquet - this is the list of relationships found in the dataset, which are the edges of the graph. - text_units.parquet - this is the source text chunks the graph was extracted from. This is optional depending on the query method you intend to use (described later).&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-04e9a4e23946a2a3618f112c8e332247</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;FILE PATH&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `BaseParser` subclass must implement the `parse` method, which is the entry point for the parsing logic. This method takes a `ParseContext` object as an argument, which contains all the necessary information about the document to be parsed, such as the document identifier, file path, and content data. The implementation of the `parse` method should handle the parsing logic and return a `ParseResult` object, which contains the parsing results and any necessary metadata.">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&quot;SOFTWARE&quot;" target="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;__](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/moving-to-graphrag-1-0-streamlining-ergonomics-for-developers-and-users)&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-fc6991d03ac28fb348468bf2d1c2d295</data>
</edge>
