</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Once a `BaseParser` subclass is implemented, the engine needs to be registered to be recognized and used by the LightRAG system. This registration is done through a `ParserSpec` object, which contains metadata about the engine such as its name, version, and supported file types. The entry point for automatic discovery of these `ParserSpec` objects is the `lightrag.parsers` directory, where third-party engines can provide their own modules that are discovered at runtime by LightRAG.&quot;" target="&quot;PARSERSPEC OBJECT&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Once a `BaseParser` subclass is implemented, the engine needs to be registered to be recognized and used by the LightRAG system. This registration is done through a `ParserSpec` object, which contains metadata about the engine such as its name, version, and supported file types. The entry point for automatic discovery of these `ParserSpec` objects is the `lightrag.parsers` directory, where third-party engines can provide their own modules that are discovered at runtime by LightRAG.&quot;" target="&quot;SUPPORTED FILE TYPES&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Once a `BaseParser` subclass is implemented, the engine needs to be registered to be recognized and used by the LightRAG system. This registration is done through a `ParserSpec` object, which contains metadata about the engine such as its name, version, and supported file types. The entry point for automatic discovery of these `ParserSpec` objects is the `lightrag.parsers` directory, where third-party engines can provide their own modules that are discovered at runtime by LightRAG.&quot;" target="&quot;LIGHTRAG.PARSERS DIRECTORY&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;Once a `BaseParser` subclass is implemented, the engine needs to be registered to be recognized and used by the LightRAG system. This registration is done through a `ParserSpec` object, which contains metadata about the engine such as its name, version, and supported file types. The entry point for automatic discovery of these `ParserSpec` objects is the `lightrag.parsers` directory, where third-party engines can provide their own modules that are discovered at runtime by LightRAG.&quot;" target="&quot;BASEPARSER SUBCLASS&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;When API upload explicitly passes `parse_engine=&quot;myengine&quot;`, that engine is fixed directly (stored in the `PENDING_PARSE` row and honored verbatim by the worker; unsupported suffixes are FAILED instead of silently falling back). Engines that register `endpoint_configured` are skipped by routing when the endpoint is not configured (hint/rule validation also shows the `endpoint_requirement` prompt);&quot;" target="&quot;TESTING RECOMMENDATIONS&quot;">
  <data key="d5">5.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-75f938ff935899b319e2b8e1890da167</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `BaseParser` subclass must implement the `parse` method, which is the entry point for the parsing logic. This method takes a `ParseContext` object as an argument, which contains all the necessary information about the document to be parsed, such as the document identifier, file path, and content data. The implementation of the `parse` method should handle the parsing logic and return a `ParseResult` object, which contains the parsing results and any necessary metadata." target="&quot;PARSING LOGIC&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `BaseParser` subclass must implement the `parse` method, which is the entry point for the parsing logic. This method takes a `ParseContext` object as an argument, which contains all the necessary information about the document to be parsed, such as the document identifier, file path, and content data. The implementation of the `parse` method should handle the parsing logic and return a `ParseResult` object, which contains the parsing results and any necessary metadata." target="&quot;PARSE METHOD&quot;">
  <data key="d5">85.0</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-befc9ee33b58f79fec1dbee2eaeae5f2</data>
</edge>
<edge source="&lt;hyperedge&gt;&quot;The `BaseParser` subclass must implement the `parse` method, which is the entry point for the parsing logic. This method takes a `ParseContext` object as an argument, which contains all the necessary information about the document to be parsed, such as the document identifier, file path, and content data. The implementation of the `parse` method should handle the parsing logic and return a `ParseResult` object, which contains the parsing results and any necessary metadata." target="&quot;PARSERESULT OBJECT&quot;">
